<p>When a delivery arrives damaged, or the supplier sent the wrong thing, record the goods going back. HardPoint takes them out of stock and
  raises a debit note, so what you owe the supplier and your VAT stay right.</p>

<h2>Recording a return</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Open the delivery (<strong>Purchasing › Receiving</strong>) and press <strong>Return goods to …</strong>. Its products are listed at what
    you paid. Or go to <strong>Purchasing › Returns › Return goods</strong> and scan the items.</li>
  <li>Enter how many of each are going back and choose the reason. For products tracked by batch, choose the batch, or leave it to take the
    one that expires first.</li>
  <li>Leave <strong>Email the debit note to the supplier</strong> ticked to send them the PDF straight away.</li>
</ol>
<p>The goods leave stock at once ("returned to supplier" in the stock history), and the debit note, which is the cost plus VAT, comes off what you owe
  the supplier. It settles their oldest invoice first, like a payment. The VAT report takes its VAT off the period's input tax.</p>

<h2>The supplier's credit note</h2>
<p>When the supplier sends their credit note, open the return and note its number and date. <strong>Purchasing › Returns</strong> lists the
  returns still waiting for one, so you can chase them. The amount was already taken off what you owe when the return was recorded, so the
  credit note doesn't reduce it again.</p>
